\section{Treat Address Change as an Object-Identity Operation}

A two-semispace collector allocates into one range while
reserving a second for the next collection. At the pause,
the occupied range becomes from-space and the empty range
becomes to-space. Copy reachable objects to to-space, repair
their incoming references, and discard from-space as a unit.
This exchanges memory headroom for a compact destination and
a pause that does not need to inspect individual garbage
objects. Each old object has at most one destination.
Preserving that property matters for mutable objects and for
reference equality: two references to one object must not
start pointing at different copies.

Forwarding is the operation that preserves sharing. If an old
header has a forwarding tag, return the recorded destination.
Otherwise read the old descriptor and size, copy the object
with its ordinary header intact, and replace the source header
with a forwarding record. A minimum object extent must be
large enough for that record, or the implementation can use
side metadata. Appel's exposition uses Cheney's traversal
and interprets the destination itself as a work queue
\cite{appel2004modern}. The Symbolics 3600 copying design
described by Moon shares the notion of forwarding but uses
different spaces and scheduling; two fixed halves are a
design choice, not a requirement of forwarding.

\section{Read the Destination as a Queue}

In \Cref{fig:visual-413}, objects before the scan cursor have
no remaining from-space references. Objects between the scan
and free cursors are parseable destination objects, but their
fields may still refer to old addresses. The unallocated
suffix after the free cursor is not scanned. This is why the
destination header must preserve the original descriptor and
extent. At every loop iteration the cursor starts at exactly
one object, not at the first pointer field.

