% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Evacuate Each Object Exactly Once}

\begin{verbatim}
forward(p):
    if p is null or outside_from_space(p): return p
    assert p is an object start
    if forwarded(p): return forwarding_address(p)
    bytes := extent(p)
    assert bytes <= to_limit - free
    dst := free
    copy_bytes(dst, p, bytes)
    free := free + bytes
    install_forwarding(p, dst)
    return dst

flip(roots):
    scan := to_begin
    free := to_begin
    for slot in roots: slot.store(forward(slot.load()))
    while scan < free:
        obj := object_at(scan)
        for slot in strong_slots(obj):
            slot.store(forward(slot.load()))
        scan := scan + extent(obj)
    assert scan = free
    exchange_space_roles_and_resume_allocation_at(free)
\end{verbatim}

The mutator is stopped and no allocation runs while
\texttt{forward} is active. If immutable or nonmoving objects
exist outside from-space, ignoring their addresses does not
eliminate their outgoing edges. Any slots in those objects
that can refer to from-space must be seeded as roots or
enumerated by a separate policy. For a generational instance see
\Cref{ch:ext-415}. Likewise, weak slots must not
call this routine indiscriminately: evacuating their referents
would convert weak reachability into strong reachability. Handle
roots or recomputation must restore derived addresses that
cannot themselves be interpreted as object starts.

\section{Check Headroom Before Entering Forwarding}

In equal-size spaces with the same alignment and no promotion
or collection-time allocation, the total extent copied is at
most the old occupied extent. Destination exhaustion then
indicates a bug or a violated assumption. But if the application
filled a 16 MiB space and 15 MiB survives, a requested 2 MiB
object still does not fit after the flip. Collection completed
correctly; capacity did not suffice. Grow or fail according
to the allocation policy rather than flipping repeatedly and
recopying the same survivors. If a variable-size planner
chooses a smaller destination, an evacuation failure after
source headers have been overwritten is not a routine retry.
It needs a planned fallback or prior reserved capacity.

Physical locality is a separate decision. Breadth-first order
puts objects discovered around the same time together, but
need not put the nodes along one hot path close to each other.
For a wide tree whose common operation follows one child per
level, a depth-first layout can pack that route more tightly
on some workloads. Conversely, a scan processing all items
on one level may benefit from breadth-first layout. Do not
attribute gains from rebuilding a contiguous heap entirely to
one traversal order. Measure bytes copied, objects copied,
post-collection allocation runway, and application access
patterns separately.

\section{Exercise the Space Boundary Twice}

Run fixtures with one self-loop, two paths to one child, and a
mixed-size cycle. Compare object identity before and after
the move using test IDs not derived from addresses. Then force
a second flip so both ranges have occupied both roles.
A stale address could coincidentally point inside the active
space on the second flip, so verify the graph before every
reuse. On debug builds invalidate or poison from-space when
the proof says no live slots go there. A parser that depends
on a stale header will then fail near the bad collection.

Finally, test exact fit in the destination and an allocation
that requires one alignment unit more. Keep a pinned or
foreign object out of this semispace unless the design has a
special place for it. Merely skipping its copy would cause
the old range to be reclaimed around it. A nonmoving region
with recorded outgoing slots accounts for both its lifetime
and any references it holds to evacuated objects.

\section{Audit the Order of Header Operations}

A forwarding tag is only recognized in the occupied portion
of from-space. The object at an arbitrary address within that
range is not necessarily valid, so a debug visitor can check
an object-start bitmap before trusting the header. Once an
object is forwarded, reading its ordinary size from that old
header is no longer meaningful; a debug heap iterator for
old space needs precomputed starts or some separate way to
decode forwarded extents. In production the basic algorithm
does not walk old space linearly, so it does not need that
additional metadata merely to advance its queue.

An implementation might optimize a byte vector by omitting
its padding from the physical copy. Its destination extent
must still include the padding, and its object header must
still be initialized. If another subsystem exposes stable
object bytes, such as hashing an explicitly specified byte
payload, it cannot read unspecified padding as part of the
logical value. Use a vector of length one together with an
object of a different alignment-sensitive extent to test the
scanner's advance. Looking only at the first relocated object
does not catch a bad queue stride.

For a heap with multiple roots, also change enumeration order
in the test harness. The new addresses and hence the order
of scanning may vary, but the graph modulo addresses must be
the same. This gives a direct way to detect hidden assumptions
that a particular root is the one to copy a shared object.
Repeat with repeated root values in different slots, rather
than registering one physical root slot several times.
